Here is an example Matlab program that uses the bisection method to determine the approximate mass of the bungee jumper:
```
% Constants
g = 9.81;  % acceleration of gravity (m/s^2)
C = 0.25;  % drag coefficient (kg/m)
v = 40;    % desired velocity (m/s)
t = 3;     % time of free fall (s)

% Initialize variables
m_low = 0;
m_high = 200;
m_approx = (m_low + m_high) / 2;
Fg = m_approx*g;       % force of gravity (N)
Fd = C*v^2;     % force of drag (N)
Fnet = Fg - Fd; % net force (N)

stopping_criterion = 0.005;

% Bisection method
while abs(Fnet) > stopping_criterion
    if Fnet < 0
        m_high = m_approx;
    else
        m_low = m_approx;
    end
    m_approx = (m_low + m_high) / 2;
    Fg = m_approx*g;
    Fd = C*v^2;
    Fnet = Fg - Fd;
end

fprintf('Approximate mass of jumper: %.4f kg\n', m_approx)
```
This program uses the bisection method to find the mass at which the net force is closest to zero, which corresponds to the desired velocity of 40 m/s after 3 seconds of free fall. It starts with an initial range of mass (m\_low, m\_high) and find the middle point of the range as an approximate mass (m\_approx). Then it calculates the net force (gravity minus drag) at that mass. If the net force is less than 0, it means the approximate mass is higher than the desired mass so it updates the m\_high to m\_approx otherwise updates the m\_low to m\_approx. The bisection method repeat the process until the net force is less than stopping criterion value. The program also prints the approximate mass for convenience.

Note that this is just an example program, the final solution may vary based on the specific requirement.
